<h1>alternation</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="modelOfComputation.html"><em>model of computation</em></a> proposed by A. K. Chandra, L. Stockmeyere, and D. Kozen, which has two kinds of states, AND and OR.  The definition of accepting computation is adjusted accordingly.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="timespace.html"><em>time/space complexity</em></a>, <a href="turingMachine.html"><em>Turing machine</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
First proposed as a model for parallel computation, it has been widely used to prove complexity bounds on problems.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorSKS">SKS</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>A. K. Chandra and L. J. Stockmeyer</strong>, <em>Alternation</em>, pages 98-108, and <strong>D. Kozen</strong>, <em>On Parallelism in Turing Machines</em>, pages 89-97, both in  Proc. Seventeenth Annual IEEE Symposium on Foundations of  Computer Science, 1976.
</p>

